[{"slug": "virginia-squatters-rights-adverse-possession", "title": "Virginia Squatters Rights and Adverse Possession: What Owners Should Know", "content": "<!-- wp:html -->\n<style>.wsp-hero{position:relative;margin:0 calc(-50vw + 50%);padding:5rem 1.5rem;background-image:none;background:linear-gradient(170deg,#12293f 0%,#0c1c2e 46%,#060c16 100%);overflow:hidden;}.wsp-hero-inner{max-width:1216px;margin:0 auto;display:grid;grid-template-columns:1.1fr 1fr;gap:2.5rem;align-items:center;}.wsp-hero-left{text-align:center;}.wsp-hero-left h1{color:#fff;font-size:2.6rem;font-weight:800;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0 1rem;text-shadow:1px 1px 2px rgba(0,0,0,.3);}.wsp-hero-left h2{color:#fff;font-weight:300;font-size:1.3rem;margin:0 0 1rem;text-shadow:1px 1px 2px rgba(0,0,0,.3);}.wsp-hero-left p{color:#fff;font-size:1rem;font-weight:300;max-width:500px;margin:0 auto 1.5rem;text-shadow:1px 1px 2px rgba(0,0,0,.3);}.wsp-btn-outline{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;border:2px solid #fff;border-radius:30px;padding:.6rem 1.4rem;font-weight:600;color:#fff;text-decoration:none;font-size:.95rem;}.wsp-btn-outline:hover{background:#fff;color:#1A202C;}.wsp-hero-right iframe{width:100%;min-height:560px;border:none;border-radius:4px;}.wsp-wrap{max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;padding:3rem 1.5rem 4rem;}@media (max-width:900px){.wsp-hero-inner{grid-template-columns:1fr;}.wsp-hero-left h1{font-size:2.1rem;}}.wsp-wrap h3{background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-left:4px solid #3182CE;border-radius:8px;padding:.9rem 1.25rem;margin:1.75rem 0 0;font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;color:#1A202C;}.wsp-wrap h3+p{background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-top:none;border-radius:0 0 8px 8px;padding:1rem 1.25rem;margin:0 0 .25rem;color:#444;}.wsp-wrap table td{vertical-align:top;}.chb-cta p{margin:0}.wsp-related{margin:2.5rem 0 1rem}.wsp-related h2{font-size:1.3rem;margin-bottom:1rem;font-weight:600;}.wsp-related-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(220px,1fr));gap:1rem;}.wsp-related-card{background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;padding:1.1rem 1.25rem;}.wsp-related-card a{font-weight:700;color:var(--global-palette1, #3182CE);text-decoration:none;}.wsp-related-card a:hover{text-decoration:underline;}.wsp-related-card p{margin:.35rem 0 0;font-size:.9rem;color:#555;}.wsp-hero a.wsp-btn-call{background:#E0A83C !important;border:2px solid #E0A83C !important;color:#16203A !important;font-size:1.25rem !important;font-weight:800 !important;padding:1rem 2.2rem !important;border-radius:40px !important;box-shadow:0 0 0 4px rgba(224,168,60,.25),0 8px 26px rgba(224,168,60,.5) !important;text-shadow:none !important;}.wsp-hero a.wsp-btn-call strong{color:#16203A !important;}.wsp-hero a.wsp-btn-call:hover{background:#fff !important;border-color:#fff !important;}</style><div class='wsp-hero'><div class='wsp-hero-inner'><div class='wsp-hero-left'><h1>Virginia Squatters Rights and Adverse Possession: What Owners Should Know</h1><div class=wsp-kicker>Cash Home Buyers in Virginia</div><div class=wsp-speed><span>Cash Offer in 24 Hours</span></div><ul class=wsp-checks><li>Virginia Squatters Rights and Adverse Possession: inherited, behind on payments, tenants or repairs, we buy the house as-is</li><li>No fees, commissions or closing costs on a Virginia Squatters Rights and Adverse Possession sale</li><li>Turn the Virginia Squatters Rights and Adverse Possession offer down and you owe us nothing</li></ul><h2>Selling a Virginia House? Get a Cash Offer</h2><p>We buy as-is.</p><p><a class='wsp-btn-outline wsp-btn-call' href='tel:424-435-2326'>Call or Text <strong>&nbsp;(424) 435-2326</strong></a></p></div><div class='wsp-hero-right'><iframe src='https://connect.cashhomebuyersca.com/widget/form/zKzKbSeB3aqwWwE8NNxO' data-form-id='zKzKbSeB3aqwWwE8NNxO' title='Cash Offer Form'></iframe><br /><script src='https://connect.cashhomebuyersca.com/js/form_embed.js' async></script></div></div></div><div class='wsp-wrap'><p><em>Last updated November 14, 2026.</em></p><p><strong>Virginia law sets out when a person occupying land can claim title by adverse possession, and how an owner can remove someone who has no right to be there.</strong></p><h2>What the official sources say</h2><ul><li>No person may make an entry on, or bring an action to recover, any land unless within fifteen years after the right first accrued (the 15-year limitation underlying adverse possession). (Va. Code 8.01-236; <a href='https://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/title8.01/chapter4/section8.01-236/' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer noopener nofollow'>source</a>)</li><li>An action for unlawful entry or detainer under 8.01-124 must be brought within three years after the entry or detainer. (Va. Code 8.01-236; <a href='https://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/title8.01/chapter4/section8.01-236/' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer noopener nofollow'>source</a>)</li><li>If land is unlawfully detained, the person entitled to possession may present a sworn statement to a magistrate or a clerk or judge of a general district court, who issues a summons for unlawful detainer; summons must be served at least 10 days before the return day. (Va. Code 8.01-126; <a href='https://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/title8.01/chapter3/section8.01-126/' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer noopener nofollow'>source</a>)</li></ul><div class=\"chb-cta\" style=\"margin:32px 0;border-radius:10px;background:linear-gradient(120deg,#0F1C2E 0%,#16293F 100%);padding:28px 32px;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:20px;flex-wrap:wrap;color:#fff;\"><div style=\"max-width:600px;\"><div style=\"font-size:12px;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:2px;color:#E0A83C;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:6px;\">Selling in Virginia?</div><div style=\"font-weight:800;font-size:20px;line-height:1.35;color:#fff;\">We buy Virginia houses as-is, with no commission, no repairs and no closing costs to you.</div></div><a href=\"#\" onclick=\"var f=document.querySelector('iframe[data-form-id]');if(f){f.scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth',block:'center'});return false;}\" style=\"flex-shrink:0;padding:14px 24px;border-radius:8px;background:#E0A83C;color:#16203A;font-size:14px;font-weight:800;text-decoration:none;white-space:nowrap;\">Get My Cash Offer &rarr;</a></div><h2>Virginia Squatters Rights and Adverse Possession FAQ</h2><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Can a squatter take ownership of my Virginia house?</h3><p> Check the statute and talk to a Virginia attorney about your facts.</p><div class='wsp-related'><div class='wsp-related-grid'><div class='wsp-related-card'><a href='https://cashhomebuyersca.com/virginia-landlord-tenant-law-tenant-rights/'>Virginia Landlord Tenant Law and Tenant Rights</a><p>Official sources.</p></div><div class='wsp-related-card'><a href='https://cashhomebuyersca.com/virginia-eviction-notice/'>Virginia Eviction Notice: Types and Days</a><p>Official sources.</p></div><div class='wsp-related-card'><a href='https://cashhomebuyersca.com/virginia-guides-by-city-and-county/'>Virginia guides by city and county</a><p>Every local guide we publish.</p></div><div class='wsp-related-card'><a href='https://cashhomebuyersca.com/we-buy-houses-in-virginia/'>We buy houses in Virginia</a><p>How a cash offer works.</p></div></div></div><h2>Sources</h2><ul><li><a href='https://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/title8.01/chapter4/section8.01-236/' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer noopener nofollow'>Va. Code 8.01-236</a></li><li><a href='https://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/title8.01/chapter3/section8.01-126/' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer noopener nofollow'>Va. Code 8.01-126</a></li></ul><p><em>General information for Virginia Squatters Rights and Adverse Possession, not legal or tax advice. Confirm details with the official office or a licensed professional.</em></p><script type=\"application/ld+json\">{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\", \"@type\": \"FAQPage\", \"mainEntity\": [{\"@type\": \"Question\", \"name\": \"Can a squatter take ownership of my Virginia house?\", \"acceptedAnswer\": {\"@type\": \"Answer\", \"text\": \"Only by meeting every requirement of the adverse possession statute cited above for the full period. Check the statute and talk to a Virginia attorney about your facts.\"}}]}</script><script type=\"application/ld+json\">{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\", \"@type\": \"BreadcrumbList\", \"itemListElement\": [{\"@type\": \"ListItem\", \"position\": 1, \"name\": \"Home\", \"item\": \"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/\"}, {\"@type\": \"ListItem\", \"position\": 2, \"name\": \"Virginia\", \"item\": \"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/we-buy-houses-in-virginia/\"}, {\"@type\": \"ListItem\", \"position\": 3, \"name\": \"Virginia Squatters Rights and Adverse Possession\", \"item\": \"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/virginia-squatters-rights-adverse-possession/\"}]}</script></div>\n<!-- /wp:html -->", "status": "publish", "rank_math_title": "Virginia Squatters Rights and Adverse Possession", "rank_math_description": "Virginia squatters rights and adverse possession: what the statutes say and how owners remove occupants or sell. Official sources.", "rank_math_focus_keyword": "virginia squatters rights"}, {"slug": "virginia-landlord-tenant-law-tenant-rights", "title": "Virginia Landlord Tenant Law and Tenant Rights: Rules for Selling a Rental", "content": "<!-- wp:html -->\n<style>.wsp-hero{position:relative;margin:0 calc(-50vw + 50%);padding:5rem 1.5rem;background-image:none;background:linear-gradient(170deg,#12293f 0%,#0c1c2e 46%,#060c16 100%);overflow:hidden;}.wsp-hero-inner{max-width:1216px;margin:0 auto;display:grid;grid-template-columns:1.1fr 1fr;gap:2.5rem;align-items:center;}.wsp-hero-left{text-align:center;}.wsp-hero-left h1{color:#fff;font-size:2.6rem;font-weight:800;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0 1rem;text-shadow:1px 1px 2px rgba(0,0,0,.3);}.wsp-hero-left h2{color:#fff;font-weight:300;font-size:1.3rem;margin:0 0 1rem;text-shadow:1px 1px 2px rgba(0,0,0,.3);}.wsp-hero-left p{color:#fff;font-size:1rem;font-weight:300;max-width:500px;margin:0 auto 1.5rem;text-shadow:1px 1px 2px rgba(0,0,0,.3);}.wsp-btn-outline{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;border:2px solid #fff;border-radius:30px;padding:.6rem 1.4rem;font-weight:600;color:#fff;text-decoration:none;font-size:.95rem;}.wsp-btn-outline:hover{background:#fff;color:#1A202C;}.wsp-hero-right iframe{width:100%;min-height:560px;border:none;border-radius:4px;}.wsp-wrap{max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;padding:3rem 1.5rem 4rem;}@media (max-width:900px){.wsp-hero-inner{grid-template-columns:1fr;}.wsp-hero-left h1{font-size:2.1rem;}}.wsp-wrap h3{background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-left:4px solid #3182CE;border-radius:8px;padding:.9rem 1.25rem;margin:1.75rem 0 0;font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;color:#1A202C;}.wsp-wrap h3+p{background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-top:none;border-radius:0 0 8px 8px;padding:1rem 1.25rem;margin:0 0 .25rem;color:#444;}.wsp-wrap table td{vertical-align:top;}.chb-cta p{margin:0}.wsp-related{margin:2.5rem 0 1rem}.wsp-related h2{font-size:1.3rem;margin-bottom:1rem;font-weight:600;}.wsp-related-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(220px,1fr));gap:1rem;}.wsp-related-card{background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;padding:1.1rem 1.25rem;}.wsp-related-card a{font-weight:700;color:var(--global-palette1, #3182CE);text-decoration:none;}.wsp-related-card a:hover{text-decoration:underline;}.wsp-related-card p{margin:.35rem 0 0;font-size:.9rem;color:#555;}.wsp-hero a.wsp-btn-call{background:#E0A83C !important;border:2px solid #E0A83C !important;color:#16203A !important;font-size:1.25rem !important;font-weight:800 !important;padding:1rem 2.2rem !important;border-radius:40px !important;box-shadow:0 0 0 4px rgba(224,168,60,.25),0 8px 26px rgba(224,168,60,.5) !important;text-shadow:none !important;}.wsp-hero a.wsp-btn-call strong{color:#16203A !important;}.wsp-hero a.wsp-btn-call:hover{background:#fff !important;border-color:#fff !important;}</style><div class='wsp-hero'><div class='wsp-hero-inner'><div class='wsp-hero-left'><h1>Virginia Landlord Tenant Law and Tenant Rights: Rules for Selling a Rental</h1><div class=wsp-kicker>Cash Home Buyers in Virginia</div><div class=wsp-speed><span>Cash Offer in 24 Hours</span></div><ul class=wsp-checks><li>Virginia Landlord Tenant Law and Tenant Rights: inherited, behind on payments, tenants or repairs, we buy the house as-is</li><li>No fees, commissions or closing costs on a Virginia Landlord Tenant Law and Tenant Rights sale</li><li>Turn the Virginia Landlord Tenant Law and Tenant Rights offer down and you owe us nothing</li></ul><h2>Selling a Virginia House? Get a Cash Offer</h2><p>We buy as-is.</p><p><a class='wsp-btn-outline wsp-btn-call' href='tel:424-435-2326'>Call or Text <strong>&nbsp;(424) 435-2326</strong></a></p></div><div class='wsp-hero-right'><iframe src='https://connect.cashhomebuyersca.com/widget/form/zKzKbSeB3aqwWwE8NNxO' data-form-id='zKzKbSeB3aqwWwE8NNxO' title='Cash Offer Form'></iframe><br /><script src='https://connect.cashhomebuyersca.com/js/form_embed.js' async></script></div></div></div><div class='wsp-wrap'><p><em>Last updated November 14, 2026.</em></p><p><strong>Virginia statutes set the basic rules between landlords and tenants, including deposits, notices and what happens when a rental is sold.</strong></p><h2>What the official sources say</h2><ul><li>Landlord may not demand or receive a security deposit exceeding two months&#x27; periodic rent. (Va. Code 55.1-1226(A); <a href='https://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/title55.1/chapter12/section55.1-1226/' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer noopener nofollow'>source</a>)</li><li>Landlord must give the tenant a written itemization of deductions, with any amount due, within 45 days after the tenancy ends or tenant vacates, whichever is last. (Va. Code 55.1-1226(A); <a href='https://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/title55.1/chapter12/section55.1-1226/' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer noopener nofollow'>source</a>)</li><li>The holder of the landlord&#x27;s interest at termination is bound by the deposit section and must return any deposit duly owed, whether or not it was transferred with the interest, regardless of contract (applies on sale of the property). (Va. Code 55.1-1226(E); <a href='https://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/title55.1/chapter12/section55.1-1226/' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer noopener nofollow'>source</a>)</li><li>Month-to-month tenancy may be terminated by either party with written notice at least 30 days before the next rent due date, unless the agreement provides otherwise; week-to-week requires 7 days. (Va. Code 55.1-1253; <a href='https://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/title55.1/chapter12/section55.1-1253/' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer noopener nofollow'>source</a>)</li><li>On a month-to-month or hold-over tenancy a new rent stated in written notice cannot take effect until the next rent due date coming 30 days after the notice. (Va. Code 55.1-1253; <a href='https://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/title55.1/chapter12/section55.1-1253/' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer noopener nofollow'>source</a>)</li><li>Landlord must give notice of intent to enter and enter only at reasonable times (except emergency or impracticable); at least 72 hours&#x27; notice for non-requested routine maintenance unless impractical. (Va. Code 55.1-1229; <a href='https://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/title55.1/chapter12/section55.1-1229/' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer noopener nofollow'>source</a>)</li><li>Landlord must comply with building and housing codes materially affecting health and safety and make all repairs necessary to keep the premises fit and habitable. (Va. Code 55.1-1220; <a href='https://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/title55.1/chapter12/section55.1-1220/' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer noopener nofollow'>source</a>)</li></ul><div class=\"chb-cta\" style=\"margin:32px 0;border-radius:10px;background:linear-gradient(120deg,#0F1C2E 0%,#16293F 100%);padding:28px 32px;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:20px;flex-wrap:wrap;color:#fff;\"><div style=\"max-width:600px;\"><div style=\"font-size:12px;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:2px;color:#E0A83C;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:6px;\">Selling in Virginia?</div><div style=\"font-weight:800;font-size:20px;line-height:1.35;color:#fff;\">We buy Virginia houses as-is, with no commission, no repairs and no closing costs to you.</div></div><a href=\"#\" onclick=\"var f=document.querySelector('iframe[data-form-id]');if(f){f.scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth',block:'center'});return false;}\" style=\"flex-shrink:0;padding:14px 24px;border-radius:8px;background:#E0A83C;color:#16203A;font-size:14px;font-weight:800;text-decoration:none;white-space:nowrap;\">Get My Cash Offer &rarr;</a></div><div class='wsp-related'><div class='wsp-related-grid'><div class='wsp-related-card'><a href='https://cashhomebuyersca.com/virginia-squatters-rights-adverse-possession/'>Virginia Squatters Rights and Adverse Possession</a><p>Official sources.</p></div><div class='wsp-related-card'><a href='https://cashhomebuyersca.com/virginia-eviction-notice/'>Virginia Eviction Notice: Types and Days</a><p>Official sources.</p></div><div class='wsp-related-card'><a href='https://cashhomebuyersca.com/virginia-guides-by-city-and-county/'>Virginia guides by city and county</a><p>Every local guide we publish.</p></div><div class='wsp-related-card'><a href='https://cashhomebuyersca.com/we-buy-houses-in-virginia/'>We buy houses in Virginia</a><p>How a cash offer works.</p></div></div></div><h2>Sources</h2><ul><li><a href='https://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/title55.1/chapter12/section55.1-1226/' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer noopener nofollow'>Va. Code 55.1-1226(A)</a></li><li><a href='https://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/title55.1/chapter12/section55.1-1253/' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer noopener nofollow'>Va. Code 55.1-1253</a></li><li><a href='https://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/title55.1/chapter12/section55.1-1229/' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer noopener nofollow'>Va. Code 55.1-1229</a></li><li><a href='https://law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/title55.1/chapter12/section55.1-1220/' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer noopener nofollow'>Va. Code 55.1-1220</a></li></ul><p><em>General information for Virginia Landlord Tenant Law and Tenant Rights, not legal or tax advice. Confirm details with the official office or a licensed professional.</em></p><script type=\"application/ld+json\">{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\", \"@type\": \"BreadcrumbList\", \"itemListElement\": [{\"@type\": \"ListItem\", \"position\": 1, \"name\": \"Home\", \"item\": \"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/\"}, {\"@type\": \"ListItem\", \"position\": 2, \"name\": \"Virginia\", \"item\": \"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/we-buy-houses-in-virginia/\"}, {\"@type\": \"ListItem\", \"position\": 3, \"name\": \"Virginia Landlord Tenant Law and Tenant Rights\", \"item\": \"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/virginia-landlord-tenant-law-tenant-rights/\"}]}</script></div>\n<!-- /wp:html -->", "status": "publish", "rank_math_title": "Virginia Landlord Tenant Law and Tenant Rights", "rank_math_description": "Virginia landlord tenant law and tenant rights: deposits, notices, entry and selling a rented house. Official sources only.", "rank_math_focus_keyword": "virginia tenant rights"}, {"slug": "virginia-eviction-notice", "title": "Virginia Eviction Notice Rules: Notice Types, Days and Selling the House", "content": "<!-- wp:html -->\n<style>.wsp-hero{position:relative;margin:0 calc(-50vw + 50%);padding:5rem 1.5rem;background-image:none;background:linear-gradient(170deg,#12293f 0%,#0c1c2e 46%,#060c16 100%);overflow:hidden;}.wsp-hero-inner{max-width:1216px;margin:0 auto;display:grid;grid-template-columns:1.1fr 1fr;gap:2.5rem;align-items:center;}.wsp-hero-left{text-align:center;}.wsp-hero-left h1{color:#fff;font-size:2.6rem;font-weight:800;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0 1rem;text-shadow: